  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":51,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A survey at a market in Porto-Novo records four variables for each of 120 traders: A the number of crates sold in a day; B the type of goods sold (tomatoes, peppers, onions, okra); C the mass of a crate in kilograms; D the market stall number.\\n\\na State which of the four variables are categorical and which are quantitative. (1)\\n\\nb Say which quantitative variables are discrete and which is continuous, giving a reason in each case. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A survey at a market in Porto-Novo records four variables for each of 120 traders: A the number of crates sold in a day; B the type of goods sold (tomatoes, peppers, onions, okra); C the mass of a crate in kilograms; D the market stall number."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a State which of the four variables are categorical and which are quantitative. (1)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Say which quantitative variables are discrete and which is continuous, giving a reason in each case. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Categorical: B (type of goods) and D (stall number — a label). Quantitative: A (crates sold) and C (mass of crate). (b) A is discrete: it is a count of crates, so only whole numbers are possible. C is continuous: a mass can take any value on a scale (e.g. 12.4 kg or 12.45 kg).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 1 mark: both pairs correctly classified (B, D categorical; A, C quantitative). D must be treated as categorical; accepting it as quantitative is a misconception to note. (b) 2 marks: A discrete + reason (1); C continuous + reason (1). Reason must reference count vs measurement; mere labelling without a reason scores 0 for that mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘quantitative: A, C’ / ‘categorical: B, D’ in either order; for (b), ‘A can only be whole numbers’ and ‘C can take any value between two limits’.","m":3,"df":"easy","tp":"Categorising Data and Stem-and-Leaf Plots","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":51,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Classify labels vs numbers first, then counts vs measurements.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Which variables are categorical and which are quantitative?","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Sort the four into labels (words) versus numbers.","Ask of each number: is it a measurement you can add, or just a code that names the stall?"],"accepted":["B and D are categorical; A and C are quantitative.","Categorical: B, D. Quantitative: A, C.","A and C quantitative; B and D categorical.","Quantitative: A and C; categorical: B and D."],"keywords":[["a and c","a, c","a&c"],["b and d","b, d","b&d"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Which quantitative variables are discrete and which is continuous? Give a reason in each case.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Discrete = countable whole numbers; continuous = measurable on a scale.","A counts whole items; C is measured, not counted."],"accepted":["A is discrete (count of crates, whole numbers); C is continuous (mass measured on a scale, any value).","A discrete, C continuous: A is a count, C can take any value such as 12.4 kg."],"keywords":[["a","crates","count","whole","number of"],["discrete"],["c","mass","continuous","any value","decimal","measure","scale"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Categorical: B (type of goods) and D (stall number — a label). Quantitative: A (crates sold) and C (mass of crate). (b) A is discrete: it is a count of crates, so only whole numbers are possible. C is continuous: a mass can take any value on a scale (e.g. 12.4 kg or 12.45 kg).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 1 mark: both pairs correctly classified (B, D categorical; A, C quantitative). D must be treated as categorical; accepting it as quantitative is a misconception to note. (b) 2 marks: A discrete + reason (1); C continuous + reason (1). Reason must reference count vs measurement; mere labelling without a reason scores 0 for that mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘quantitative: A, C’ / ‘categorical: B, D’ in either order; for (b), ‘A can only be whole numbers’ and ‘C can take any value between two limits’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Categorical: B (type of goods) and D (stall number — a label). Quantitative: A (crates sold) and C (mass of crate). (b) A is discrete: it is a count of crates, so only whole numbers are possible. C is continuous: a mass can take any value on a scale (e.g. 12.4 kg or 12.45 kg).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 1 mark: both pairs correctly classified (B, D categorical; A, C quantitative). D must be treated as categorical; accepting it as quantitative is a misconception to note. (b) 2 marks: A discrete + reason (1); C continuous + reason (1). Reason must reference count vs measurement; mere labelling without a reason scores 0 for that mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘quantitative: A, C’ / ‘categorical: B, D’ in either order; for (b), ‘A can only be whole numbers’ and ‘C can take any value between two limits’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Categorical: B (type of goods) and D (stall number — a label). Quantitative: A (crates sold) and C (mass of crate). (b) A is discrete: it is a count of crates, so only whole numbers are possible. C is continuous: a mass can take any value on a scale (e.g. 12.4 kg or 12.45 kg).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 1 mark: both pairs correctly classified (B, D categorical; A, C quantitative). D must be treated as categorical; accepting it as quantitative is a misconception to note. (b) 2 marks: A discrete + reason (1); C continuous + reason (1). Reason must reference count vs measurement; mere labelling without a reason scores 0 for that mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘quantitative: A, C’ / ‘categorical: B, D’ in either order; for (b), ‘A can only be whole numbers’ and ‘C can take any value between two limits’."},{"n":52,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 15 customers at a bank in Cotonou are 12, 4, 9, 15, 7, 21, 9, 6, 11, 14, 8, 9, 17, 5, 10.\\n\\na Write the five-point summary of these times, and calculate the interquartile range. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 15 customers at a bank in Cotonou are 12, 4, 9, 15, 7, 21, 9, 6, 11, 14, 8, 9, 17, 5, 10."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Write the five-point summary of these times, and calculate the interquartile range. (2)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — Sorted: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21. n = 15; median (8th value) = 9. Lower half 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 → Q1 = 6. Upper half 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21 → Q3 = 14. Five-point summary: 4, 6, 9, 14, 21. IQR = 14 − 6 = 8 minutes.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the five-point summary (all five values, in order). 1 mark for IQR = 8 (minutes). Method marks: if the median is wrong but the halves are correctly identified and averaged, award the IQR mark only when the subtraction is consistent with the candidate’s own quartiles.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — summary written with any separators; ‘range 17, IQR 8’ if both requested; IQR in minutes.","m":2,"df":"easy","tp":"Quartiles, Range and Interquartile Range","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":52,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Sort first, then read the middle values.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) five-point summary","prompt":"Write the five-point summary (minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum).","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Order the fifteen times from smallest to largest.","Median = 8th value; Q1 = median of the lowest seven; Q3 = median of the highest seven."],"accepted":["4, 6, 9, 14, 21","4 6 9 14 21","4,6,9,14,21","min 4, Q1 6, median 9, Q3 14, max 21"],"keywords":[["4"],["6"],["9"],["14"],["21"]],"minKeywordGroups":5},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) IQR","prompt":"Calculate the interquartile range.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["IQR = Q3 − Q1.","Q3 − Q1 = 14 − 6."],"answer":8,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"minutes"}],"marking":"Model answer — Sorted: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21. n = 15; median (8th value) = 9. Lower half 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 → Q1 = 6. Upper half 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21 → Q3 = 14. Five-point summary: 4, 6, 9, 14, 21. IQR = 14 − 6 = 8 minutes.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the five-point summary (all five values, in order). 1 mark for IQR = 8 (minutes). Method marks: if the median is wrong but the halves are correctly identified and averaged, award the IQR mark only when the subtraction is consistent with the candidate’s own quartiles.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — summary written with any separators; ‘range 17, IQR 8’ if both requested; IQR in minutes.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — Sorted: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21. n = 15; median (8th value) = 9. Lower half 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 → Q1 = 6. Upper half 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21 → Q3 = 14. Five-point summary: 4, 6, 9, 14, 21. IQR = 14 − 6 = 8 minutes.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the five-point summary (all five values, in order). 1 mark for IQR = 8 (minutes). Method marks: if the median is wrong but the halves are correctly identified and averaged, award the IQR mark only when the subtraction is consistent with the candidate’s own quartiles.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — summary written with any separators; ‘range 17, IQR 8’ if both requested; IQR in minutes.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — Sorted: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21. n = 15; median (8th value) = 9. Lower half 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 → Q1 = 6. Upper half 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, 21 → Q3 = 14. Five-point summary: 4, 6, 9, 14, 21. IQR = 14 − 6 = 8 minutes.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the five-point summary (all five values, in order). 1 mark for IQR = 8 (minutes). Method marks: if the median is wrong but the halves are correctly identified and averaged, award the IQR mark only when the subtraction is consistent with the candidate’s own quartiles.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — summary written with any separators; ‘range 17, IQR 8’ if both requested; IQR in minutes."},{"n":53,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The table shows the masses, in kilograms, of 40 sacks of maize weighed at a depot in Bohicon.\\n\\na State the modal class, and estimate the mean mass of a sack to 2 decimal places. (2)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table shows the masses, in kilograms, of 40 sacks of maize weighed at a depot in Bohicon."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a State the modal class, and estimate the mean mass of a sack to 2 decimal places. (2)"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mass m (kg)"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Frequency"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 ≤ m < 15"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"15 ≤ m < 20"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20 ≤ m < 25"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"14"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25 ≤ m < 30"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Modal class: 20 ≤ m < 25 (frequency 14). Midpoints 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5 kg. Σfx = 12.5×6 + 17.5×12 + 22.5×14 + 27.5×8 = 75 + 210 + 315 + 220 = 820 kg. Estimated mean = 820 ÷ 40 = 20.5 kg (20.50 kg).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the modal class (frequency 14 named or identified). 1 mark for the estimated mean 20.5 kg (accept 20.5 or 20.50; tolerance ±0.005). If midpoints are wrong but the arithmetic on those midpoints is consistent, award 1 of 1 for the mean only if the method (Σfx ÷ n) is shown; no method, no mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘20–25 kg’; mean given as 20.50 kg.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"Grouped Frequency Tables","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":53,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Modal class = biggest frequency; mean = Σfx ÷ n with midpoints.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) modal class","prompt":"State the modal class.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["The modal class has the greatest frequency.","Compare the frequencies 6, 12, 14, 8."],"accepted":["20 ≤ m < 25","20≤m<25","20 ≤ m < 25 kg","20 to 25 kg","20-25 kg"],"keywords":[["20","25"]],"minKeywordGroups":1},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) estimated mean","prompt":"Estimate the mean mass of a sack.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Use class midpoints: 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5.","Multiply each midpoint by its class frequency, add the products, divide by 40."],"answer":20.5,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"kg"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Modal class: 20 ≤ m < 25 (frequency 14). Midpoints 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5 kg. Σfx = 12.5×6 + 17.5×12 + 22.5×14 + 27.5×8 = 75 + 210 + 315 + 220 = 820 kg. Estimated mean = 820 ÷ 40 = 20.5 kg (20.50 kg).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the modal class (frequency 14 named or identified). 1 mark for the estimated mean 20.5 kg (accept 20.5 or 20.50; tolerance ±0.005). If midpoints are wrong but the arithmetic on those midpoints is consistent, award 1 of 1 for the mean only if the method (Σfx ÷ n) is shown; no method, no mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘20–25 kg’; mean given as 20.50 kg.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Modal class: 20 ≤ m < 25 (frequency 14). Midpoints 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5 kg. Σfx = 12.5×6 + 17.5×12 + 22.5×14 + 27.5×8 = 75 + 210 + 315 + 220 = 820 kg. Estimated mean = 820 ÷ 40 = 20.5 kg (20.50 kg).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the modal class (frequency 14 named or identified). 1 mark for the estimated mean 20.5 kg (accept 20.5 or 20.50; tolerance ±0.005). If midpoints are wrong but the arithmetic on those midpoints is consistent, award 1 of 1 for the mean only if the method (Σfx ÷ n) is shown; no method, no mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘20–25 kg’; mean given as 20.50 kg.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Modal class: 20 ≤ m < 25 (frequency 14). Midpoints 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5 kg. Σfx = 12.5×6 + 17.5×12 + 22.5×14 + 27.5×8 = 75 + 210 + 315 + 220 = 820 kg. Estimated mean = 820 ÷ 40 = 20.5 kg (20.50 kg).\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for the modal class (frequency 14 named or identified). 1 mark for the estimated mean 20.5 kg (accept 20.5 or 20.50; tolerance ±0.005). If midpoints are wrong but the arithmetic on those midpoints is consistent, award 1 of 1 for the mean only if the method (Σfx ÷ n) is shown; no method, no mark.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘20–25 kg’; mean given as 20.50 kg."},{"n":54,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Thirteen employees of a company in Cotonou take 8, 11, 9, 14, 10, 12, 11, 13, 9, 10, 12, 11 and 41 minutes to travel to work. The value 41 is a data-entry error: the correct time is 14 minutes.\\n\\na Calculate the mean before and after the correction, to 2 decimal places. (2)\\n\\nb Calculate the standard deviation before the correction, to 2 decimal places. (1)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thirteen employees of a company in Cotonou take 8, 11, 9, 14, 10, 12, 11, 13, 9, 10, 12, 11 and 41 minutes to travel to work. The value 41 is a data-entry error: the correct time is 14 minutes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a Calculate the mean before and after the correction, to 2 decimal places. (2)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Calculate the standard deviation before the correction, to 2 decimal places. (1)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — Sum before = 171 → mean = 171 ÷ 13 ≈ 13.15 minutes. Corrected sum = 171 − 41 + 14 = 144 → mean = 144 ÷ 13 ≈ 11.08 minutes. Before the correction, with x̄ ≈ 13.154: Σ(x − x̄)² ≈ 873.23; variance ≈ 67.17; σ ≈ 8.20 minutes. (After the correction σ ≈ 1.82 minutes — not asked, but a useful cross-check for the examiner.)\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark each for 13.15 and 11.08 (±0.01). 1 mark for σ ≈ 8.20 (±0.02), population formula. If a candidate uses the sample formula (n − 1), σ ≈ 8.52 — accept only if the working clearly states the population formula is not used; otherwise note and award the mark at the examiner’s discretion with reason recorded. Method without the final value: award half the mark for a fully correct set-up with an arithmetic slip.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 13.2 and 11.1 to 1 d.p.; ‘13.15 min’ etc.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"Mean and Standard Deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":54,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Replace the faulty value first; then recompute.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) mean before","prompt":"Calculate the mean before the correction.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Add the thirteen times first (the 41 is included).","Divide the total by the number of employees."],"answer":13.15,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) mean after","prompt":"Calculate the mean after the correction.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Replace 41 with 14 in the list.","Form the corrected total first, then divide by the same count."],"answer":11.08,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b) σ before","prompt":"Calculate the standard deviation before the correction, using the population formula.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Use the population formula with n = 13.","Square each deviation from the mean, sum them, divide by 13, then root."],"answer":8.2,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"minutes"}],"marking":"Model answer — Sum before = 171 → mean = 171 ÷ 13 ≈ 13.15 minutes. Corrected sum = 171 − 41 + 14 = 144 → mean = 144 ÷ 13 ≈ 11.08 minutes. Before the correction, with x̄ ≈ 13.154: Σ(x − x̄)² ≈ 873.23; variance ≈ 67.17; σ ≈ 8.20 minutes. (After the correction σ ≈ 1.82 minutes — not asked, but a useful cross-check for the examiner.)\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark each for 13.15 and 11.08 (±0.01). 1 mark for σ ≈ 8.20 (±0.02), population formula. If a candidate uses the sample formula (n − 1), σ ≈ 8.52 — accept only if the working clearly states the population formula is not used; otherwise note and award the mark at the examiner’s discretion with reason recorded. Method without the final value: award half the mark for a fully correct set-up with an arithmetic slip.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 13.2 and 11.1 to 1 d.p.; ‘13.15 min’ etc.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — Sum before = 171 → mean = 171 ÷ 13 ≈ 13.15 minutes. Corrected sum = 171 − 41 + 14 = 144 → mean = 144 ÷ 13 ≈ 11.08 minutes. Before the correction, with x̄ ≈ 13.154: Σ(x − x̄)² ≈ 873.23; variance ≈ 67.17; σ ≈ 8.20 minutes. (After the correction σ ≈ 1.82 minutes — not asked, but a useful cross-check for the examiner.)\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark each for 13.15 and 11.08 (±0.01). 1 mark for σ ≈ 8.20 (±0.02), population formula. If a candidate uses the sample formula (n − 1), σ ≈ 8.52 — accept only if the working clearly states the population formula is not used; otherwise note and award the mark at the examiner’s discretion with reason recorded. Method without the final value: award half the mark for a fully correct set-up with an arithmetic slip.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 13.2 and 11.1 to 1 d.p.; ‘13.15 min’ etc.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — Sum before = 171 → mean = 171 ÷ 13 ≈ 13.15 minutes. Corrected sum = 171 − 41 + 14 = 144 → mean = 144 ÷ 13 ≈ 11.08 minutes. Before the correction, with x̄ ≈ 13.154: Σ(x − x̄)² ≈ 873.23; variance ≈ 67.17; σ ≈ 8.20 minutes. (After the correction σ ≈ 1.82 minutes — not asked, but a useful cross-check for the examiner.)\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark each for 13.15 and 11.08 (±0.01). 1 mark for σ ≈ 8.20 (±0.02), population formula. If a candidate uses the sample formula (n − 1), σ ≈ 8.52 — accept only if the working clearly states the population formula is not used; otherwise note and award the mark at the examiner’s discretion with reason recorded. Method without the final value: award half the mark for a fully correct set-up with an arithmetic slip.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 13.2 and 11.1 to 1 d.p.; ‘13.15 min’ etc."},{"n":55,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The masses of cabbages at a farm near Ouémé are normally distributed with mean 1.80 kg and standard deviation 0.25 kg.\\n\\na State the percentage of cabbages with a mass between 1.55 kg and 2.05 kg, and name the rule you used. (1)\\n\\nb A cabbage has a z-score of −2.4. Find its mass. (1)","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The masses of cabbages at a farm near Ouémé are normally distributed with mean 1.80 kg and standard deviation 0.25 kg."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"a State the percentage of cabbages with a mass between 1.55 kg and 2.05 kg, and name the rule you used. (1)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b A cabbage has a z-score of −2.4. Find its mass. (1)"}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) 1.55 = 1.80 − 0.25 = μ − σ and 2.05 = 1.80 + 0.25 = μ + σ, so the range is μ ± 1σ. By the 68–95–99.7 rule, about 68% of cabbages lie in this range. (b) mass = μ + zσ = 1.80 − 2.4 × 0.25 = 1.80 − 0.60 = 1.20 kg.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈68% with the rule named or correctly implied (accept ‘68 percent’, ‘two-thirds’, ‘the empirical rule’). 1 mark for 1.20 kg. A candidate writing 68 without naming the rule: award the mark only if the working shows μ − σ and μ + σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.2 kg; ‘about 68%’.","m":2,"df":"easy","tp":"The Normal Distribution","cfg":{"source":"myp4-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":55,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Recognise μ ± σ, then use mass = μ + zσ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) percentage and rule","prompt":"State the percentage of cabbages between 1.55 kg and 2.05 kg, and name the rule.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Write 1.55 and 2.05 as μ ± σ.","The 68–95–99.7 rule gives the area within one σ."],"accepted":["68% — the 68-95-99.7 rule","68% (68-95-99.7 rule)","68 %, 68-95-99.7 (empirical) rule","about 68%, the 68–95–99.7 rule"],"keywords":[["68"],["68-95-99.7","68–95–99.7","empirical","three sigma","68 95 99.7"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b","label":"(b) mass from z","prompt":"A cabbage has a z-score of −2.4. Find its mass.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Mass = μ + z × σ.","Replace μ, z and σ with the values from the question, then compute."],"answer":1.2,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"kg"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) 1.55 = 1.80 − 0.25 = μ − σ and 2.05 = 1.80 + 0.25 = μ + σ, so the range is μ ± 1σ. By the 68–95–99.7 rule, about 68% of cabbages lie in this range. (b) mass = μ + zσ = 1.80 − 2.4 × 0.25 = 1.80 − 0.60 = 1.20 kg.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈68% with the rule named or correctly implied (accept ‘68 percent’, ‘two-thirds’, ‘the empirical rule’). 1 mark for 1.20 kg. A candidate writing 68 without naming the rule: award the mark only if the working shows μ − σ and μ + σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.2 kg; ‘about 68%’.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) 1.55 = 1.80 − 0.25 = μ − σ and 2.05 = 1.80 + 0.25 = μ + σ, so the range is μ ± 1σ. By the 68–95–99.7 rule, about 68% of cabbages lie in this range. (b) mass = μ + zσ = 1.80 − 2.4 × 0.25 = 1.80 − 0.60 = 1.20 kg.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈68% with the rule named or correctly implied (accept ‘68 percent’, ‘two-thirds’, ‘the empirical rule’). 1 mark for 1.20 kg. A candidate writing 68 without naming the rule: award the mark only if the working shows μ − σ and μ + σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.2 kg; ‘about 68%’.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) 1.55 = 1.80 − 0.25 = μ − σ and 2.05 = 1.80 + 0.25 = μ + σ, so the range is μ ± 1σ. By the 68–95–99.7 rule, about 68% of cabbages lie in this range. (b) mass = μ + zσ = 1.80 − 2.4 × 0.25 = 1.80 − 0.60 = 1.20 kg.\\n\\nMarking — 1 mark for ≈68% with the rule named or correctly implied (accept ‘68 percent’, ‘two-thirds’, ‘the empirical rule’). 1 mark for 1.20 kg. A candidate writing 68 without naming the rule: award the mark only if the working shows μ − σ and μ + σ.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — 1.2 kg; ‘about 68%’."}]'::jsonb) as x
